[{:seon.task/name "the-record-is-an-atom"
  :seon.task/plan "The agent's record is one map it reads with @ctx (seon.ctx/ctx; notes/design-ctx-atom-2026-10-25.md, the owner's rulings of 2026-10-25). What exists: an agent reads its record through my.db queries and my.task functions, each a read of its own; the page draws its agent from the walk (seon.render/walk). The first slice (the owner, 2026-10-25: \"just deref'ing the @ctx will trigger the web ui rendering which the agent will also see so focus on the rendering functions part\"): seon.ctx builds the agent entity whole and its units (:seon.render/units) by short keys once, when the agent's state first holds its db, and patches it from each transaction's datoms (seon.agent/transact! records them), so a read is a deref and a write costs its change. The model reads @ctx through the one printer as any value; the page draws the same eval as a block (:seon.ctx/ctx, seon.render.ui/ctx), each key by the render its own schema declares, so an agent adds a datum and its render and sees both at its next eval. Writes through swap! (the validator, the basis check), provenance, the diff from main and its merge mark follow, each its own step of the note's plan."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction seon.ctx-test/the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session seon.ctx-test/a-key-renders-by-its-own-schema seon.ctx-test/an-agent-adds-a-datum-and-its-render seon.ctx-test/the-agents-state-keeps-ctx seon.agent-test/a-parked-agent-draws-its-parts-and-a-gone-one-answers-at-once seon.ctx-test/a-swap-writes-the-record seon.ctx-test/a-write-costs-its-change-not-the-record}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-change-names-its-eval"
  :seon.task/plan "Who made each fact of the record is a query on its transaction's tx-meta (notes/design-ctx-atom-2026-10-25.md §9; docs/ctx.md#provenance-who-made-each-fact). What existed: every transaction names its branch (:seon.tx/branch, seon.db/tx-meta), but a catch-up (seon.agent/merge-main!) wrote main's facts under the agent's branch alone, and nothing named the eval whose code wrote a fact, which is a separate transaction from the eval's record. The change, at the producer: seon.db/tx-meta takes :seon.tx/eval and :seon.tx/merged; seon.repl/eval-string holds the running eval's id on the agent's state, which seon.agent/transact! writes; merge-main! names main. seon.ctx/made-by reads it for one attribute, and the page's ctx block (seon.render.ui/ctx) shows it beside each key. Replaces nothing (no producer check at write: the owner's ruling of 2026-10-25, approval is the merge)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ctx/the-record-is-an-atom"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/a-change-names-its-eval seon.agent-test/an-agents-transactions-name-their-runtime seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-derivation"
  :seon.task/plan "Slice 1 of the one-derivation design (notes/one-derivation-joint-design.md, revision 3, root's approval of 2026-10-26): @ctx is the one derivation, and the page, the request and the agent's own view are its renders. This slice is ctx's side (docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt). seon.ctx/build takes any entity (an agent's, root's, the user's, a task's), and seon.ctx/follow patches it from the writer's commits of main. seon.ctx/patch answers :changed, the paths a transaction changed, keeping every other path identical? for a part's memo. ctx holds the db's data only (owner, 2026-10-26: no derived data in the db or the ctx atom): the task's data (seon.context/task-data, its memo key task-key), related (seon.context/related) and an entry's first transaction (seon.context/first-t) are renders a view reads the db through (seon.context/renders); a catch-up is a fact of the agent's record. It replaces nothing yet: the walk and the request's own reads are deleted by later slices, once views read ctx."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ctx/the-record-is-an-atom"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction seon.ctx-test/any-entitys-record-follows-mains-commits seon.ctx-test/the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session seon.context-test/the-task-key-moves-with-what-it-reads seon.context-cost-test/a-task-key-costs-what-the-plan-names seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-record-is-pulled-by-its-pattern"
  :seon.task/plan "The render build's step 2 (notes/render-design-2026-10-26.md, the owner's go 2026-10-26): what ctx holds is a pull pattern held as data, `:seon.ctx/pattern`, on each entity schema (the agent's, a run's, a task's, a var's, root's) and, edited, on the agent's own entity (docs/ctx.md#the-pattern). One walk (seon.ctx/build) and its patch (seon.ctx/patch: each touched entity rebuilt where it is held, every other subtree the same object) replace the schemas' units (:seon.render/units, deleted; seon.render/units reads a pattern's joins for the page's walk). Windows hold the newest n (:limit with :reverse, through Datahike's :reverse pull option for ids issued in time order; by the ref's transaction for content-derived ids). A task's cited vars and proofs join by name (:by :var/name, the references staying the author's symbols), and a cited var's callers by the same lookup reversed, a window. Every entity is held once (the task under the newest run). :seon.agent/answered-t, written by every eval and read by nothing, is deleted. Measured flat in the session at 100 and 1,000 evals: a new eval 22 calls, a redefinition 20, a new caller 18, a plan edit 8."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction seon.ctx-test/the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session seon.ctx-test/a-lookups-patch-costs-its-change seon.db-test/the-newest-n-cost-what-they-hold}} {:seon.task/name "share-assocs-what-it-shares"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (Jolt f561902a pinned: sorted-map assoc replaces by identical?). seon.ctx/share assocs into a fresh build only the subtrees that differ from the record's (reduce-kv), its into-a-new-map workaround deleted. With assoc fixed, a member whose ref was retracted and asserted back came back a new object of equal value (main's ctx-test red): seon.ctx/node now keeps each re-pulled attribute equal to before and is the old node when every value is the same object."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction seon.ctx-test/a-fresh-build-shares-what-it-did-not-change seon.ctx-test/a-ref-asserted-back-leaves-its-member-as-it-was}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-diff-skips-the-shared-tree"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.ctx/changes costs a step past every member of a collection it enters, though one member changed: it walks the two collections' seqs in step (jolt/a-diff-walks-collections-in-step), and Jolt's sorted-map seq allocates 193 bytes an entry on each side, so one member changed of 1,000 costs 392 KB and of 4,000 1.55 MB (seon.ctx/change-steps :entry). Measured alternatives over a sorted map of 1,000 to 2,000 entries: its seq 193 B and 18 ticks an entry, its iterator 321 B and 118 ticks, reduce-kv 479 B and 439 ticks, reduce-kv with a get into the other side 1.6 KB. A patch keeps every member it did not reach the same object, and assoc and dissoc share the tree's untouched nodes, so a diff of two sorted maps that skips a node both trees share costs O(changed members × log n). The cause is in the dependency: a Jolt function over two of its sorted maps answering the entries that differ (on a jolt/<topic> branch, the fork seantempesta/jolt), which seon.ctx/changes calls for a collection pair in place of its seqs. Not now (root, 2026-10-26: stability first; 392 KB at 1,000 members blocks nothing). Proof: seon.ctx-test/changes-step-costs-are-current with :entry near zero for a member that is the same object, and seon.ctx-test/changes-answers-what-every-pair-walked-answers unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/changes-step-costs-are-current seon.ctx-test/changes-answers-what-every-pair-walked-answers}}]
